WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 76: The Battle of Blair Mountain: The Armed Miners' Army of 1921

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.000
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.360
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:19.920
[Rebecca] In late August 1921, beneath the rugged ridges of Logan County, West Virginia, the largest armed insurrection in the United States since the Civil War erupted into open warfare.

4
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:36.720
[Rebecca] Over 10,000 Union coal miners, black, white, and newly arrived European immigrants, tied red bandanas around their necks, grabbed hunting rifles and surplus military firearms, and marched into the mountains to overthrow the brutal company town dictatorship of the coal operators.

5
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:47.920
[Rebecca] For one terrifying week, they fought a pitched military battle against 3,000 armed deputies, state troopers, and private airplanes dropping homemade poison gas and pipe bombs.

6
00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:55.200
[Rebecca] George brings us the heroic, harrowing story of the Battle of Blair Mountain.

7
00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:13.360
[George] Billions of tons of high-grade bituminous coal fueled the expanding factories, steamships, and electrical plants of industrial America.

8
00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:19.520
[George] But the miners who dug that black gold from the earth lived in conditions resembling medieval serfdom.

9
00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:31.440
[George] Coal operators owned everything, the miners' uninsulated wooden shacks, the schools, the churches, the drinking wells, and the company stores where prices were marked up by 60%.

10
00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:41.360
[George] Miners were paid not in federal currency, but in company scrip, worthless stamped brass and paper tokens that could only be redeemed at company-owned commissaries.

11
00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:48.240
[George] To ensure that miners never organized, operators employed the notorious Baldwinfeld's detective agency.

12
00:01:48.240 --> 00:02:07.560
[George] A private army of ruthless mercenaries who patrolled the camps with Winchester rifles, beat Union sympathizers, evicted striking families into snowy winter ditches, and used an armored railroad car mounted with machine guns known as the Bull Moose Special to terrorize tent colonies of displaced women and children.

13
00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:20.280
[George] Miners worked in subterranean tunnels barely four feet high, kneeling in standing water for twelve hours a day while inhaling toxic coal dust that doomed thousands to early graves from black lung disease.

14
00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:34.520
[George] Coal operators ruthlessly deducted wages for tools, blasting powder, lamp oil, and medical fees, often leaving miners with negative pay balances that plunged their families into inescapable debt bondage to the company store.

15
00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:43.000
[George] The fuse that ignited Blair Mountain was lit on May 19, 1920, in the small railroad town of Maitwin in Mingo County.

16
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:51.240
[George] A squad of thirteen Baldwinfeltz detectives, led by agency founder Lee Feltz, arrived to evict striking minor families.

17
00:02:51.240 --> 00:03:02.360
[George] But Maitwin's charismatic young police chief, Sid Hatfield, a former miner who openly supported the United Mine workers, refused to let the detectives bully his town.

18
00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:11.560
[George] Backed by town mayor Cable Testerman and armed miners watching from storefront doorways, Hatfield confronted the detectives on the railway platform.

19
00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:15.320
[George] An argument over legal warrants escalated into gunfire.

20
00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:24.600
[George] When the smoke cleared, two miners, Mayor Testerman, and seven Baldwinfeltz detectives lay dead on the gravel, including two Feltz brothers.

21
00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:33.720
[George] Sid Hatfield became an instant folk hero across the Appalachian coal fields, a fearless symbol of working-class defiance against corporate tyranny.

22
00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.840
[George] But the coal operators swore bloody vengeance.

23
00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:50.720
[George] On August 1, 1921, as Hatfield walked unarmed up the McDowell County Courthouse steps in Welch to face trumped-up charges, Baldwin Felt's gunmen shot him down in cold blood on the courthouse steps in broad daylight.

24
00:03:50.960 --> 00:04:03.920
[George] The Maitwin gunfight lasted barely 90 seconds, but it unleashed an earthquake across the Appalachian coal valleys, proving that mountain miners would no longer suffer company violence without retaliating in kind.

25
00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:15.600
[George] Sid Hatfield's funeral drew thousands of weeping miners and their wives, who covered his casket in wild mountain laurel and swore an ironclad oath to continue his fight for basic human dignity.

26
00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:20.640
[George] The brazen assassination of Sid Hatfield shattered the miners' remaining patients.

27
00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:28.720
[George] Across the hollows of Canoha, Boone and Fayette counties, church bells told and thousands of miners laid down their pickaxes.

28
00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:35.040
[George] At a mass rally in Charleston, passionate labor organizer Frank Keaney delivered an ultimatum.

29
00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:39.280
[George] You have no courts, no law, no constitutional rights.

30
00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:42.080
[George] The only way you can get them is to fight for them.

31
00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:56.240
[George] From across the hills, over 10,000 miners assembled at Lens Creek, armed with deer rifles, shotguns, Springfield 30-caliber military rifles brought home from the trenches of World War I, and boxes of dynamite.

32
00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:05.040
[George] They tied bright red bandanas around their necks as an identification marker in the dense forest, proudly coining the historic term rednecks.

33
00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:13.200
[George] This was not a disorderly mob, but a disciplined citizen army composed of World War I combat veterans who organized into platoons.

34
00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:23.840
[George] Appointed squad leaders, established medical triage stations staffed by volunteer nurses, and requisitioned supply trucks to transport food and ammunition to the front.

35
00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:37.240
[George] Frank Keaney and Mother Jones had spent years crisscrossing the southern West Virginia coalfields, dodging company gunmen and delivering fiery stump speeches to secret union meetings held deep inside abandoned mine portals.

36
00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:50.600
[George] The miners' army marched in disciplined columns along the dusty country roads, accompanied by fife and drum corps playing battle hymns from the Civil War while farm families lined the roads offering fresh biscuits and spring water.

37
00:05:50.600 --> 00:06:03.560
[George] Miners wore red bandanas knotted around their work shirts, turning the humble cotton kerchief into an international symbol of working-class solidarity that was celebrated in labor songs and picket lines across North America.

38
00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:10.680
[George] Standing between the miners' army and the captive coal camps of Logan County was Logan County Sheriff Don Chaffin.

39
00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:24.440
[George] Chaffin was the undisputed king of the coal operators, receiving tens of thousands of dollars in secret monthly payoffs from the Logan County Coal Operators Association to keep his county entirely free of Union agitators.

40
00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:36.520
[George] Chaffin mobilized a force of 3,000 men, county deputies, state police, mine managers, and armed civilian volunteers recruited from affluent neighborhoods in Huntington and Charleston.

41
00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:40.840
[George] Chaffin understood the mountainous topography of West Virginia perfectly.

42
00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:56.760
[George] He established heavily fortified defensive lines along the twelve-mile crest of Blair Mountain, digging deep trenches, setting up sandbagged machine gun nests on strategic rocky knobs, and stationing sharpshooters with high-powered rifles along every mountain gap.

43
00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:07.160
[George] Chaffin swore that no Union miner would set foot in Logan County alive, declaring that his machine guns would paint the mountain with miners' blood before he surrendered his authority.

44
00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:19.040
[George] Chaffin had turned Logan County into a fortified police state, posting armed guards at every railroad station and mountain pass to intercept Union organizers and confiscate pro-labor literature.

45
00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:33.200
[George] Coal companies paid Chaffin a commission of ten cents for every ton of coal shipped out of the county, filling his personal bank accounts with hundreds of thousands of dollars that he used to purchase weapons, ammunition, and political influence.

46
00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:40.480
[George] On August 25, 1921, the miners reached the base of Blair Mountain and the battle began in earnest.

47
00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:48.400
[George] The dense rhododendron thickets and oak groves echoed with the relentless rattle of machine guns and the crack of thousands of rifles.

48
00:07:48.400 --> 00:08:02.240
[George] For five grueling days, the miners launched wave after wave of tactical assaults up the steep, rocky slopes, attempting to flank Chaffin's entrenched machine gun bunkers at Blair Gap, Crooked Creek, and Beach Creek.

49
00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:15.680
[George] Miners who had survived the Battle of the Argonne Forest in France used trench warfare tactics, crawling through ravines, providing covering rifle fire, and using dynamite bundles to blast company gun emplacements.

50
00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:26.880
[George] Casualties mounted on both sides, with heroic volunteer miners carrying wounded comrades down muddy hillsides to improvised field hospitals established in rural schoolhouses.

51
00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:38.960
[George] Despite being outgunned by heavy water-cooled machine guns, the miners' superior numbers and combat experience began pushing Chaffin's defenders back, inching ever closer to the mountain crest.

52
00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:49.680
[George] The fighting was particularly savage at Blair Gap, where miners crawled through dense undergrowth under continuous machine gun fire to storm company entrenchments on the Rocky Ridge.

53
00:08:49.680 --> 00:09:03.160
[George] World War I veterans directed mortar fire using hollow cast iron pipes packed with black powder, demonstrating tactical combat skills that repeatedly neutralized company machine gun positions along the mountain spurs.

54
00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:16.120
[George] The fighting on the ridge was intense and unrelenting, with both sides constructing earthen breastworks and log fortifications that transformed the forested slopes of Logan County into an active military combat zone.

55
00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:27.960
[George] Desperate as his defensive lines began to fracture, Don Chaffin authorized an extraordinary, unprecedented tactic aerial warfare against American citizens on domestic soil.

56
00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:36.600
[George] Chaffin hired private biplanes, operating from a makeshift airstrip in Logan, to conduct bombing raids over the miners' positions.

57
00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:52.680
[George] Pilots and spotters flew low over the wooded ridges of Blair Mountain, dropping homemade aerial explosives, metal pipe bombs packed with black powder, nails and shrapnel, as well as surplus World War I gas bombs containing chlorine and tear gas.

58
00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:59.160
[George] While the crude bombs caused few direct fatalities because they often detonated high in the tree branches.

59
00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:14.200
[George] The terrifying psychological impact of being bombed from the sky by private coal company aircraft enraged the miners and shocked the conscience of the nation when newspaper headlines revealed that chemical weapons were being deployed against American citizens in West Virginia.

60
00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:27.480
[George] Eyewitnesses reported seeing bipeles bank low over the forested mountain passes, with observers dropping heavy cast iron bombs that tore through oak branches and exploded with concussive roars that shook the valley floor.

61
00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:39.560
[George] The deploy of aerial bombs against striking workers was denounced on the floor of the United States Senate, with progressive lawmakers demanding a federal investigation into the lawless tyranny of the coal barons.

62
00:10:39.560 --> 00:10:44.520
[George] Recognizing that the battle was escalating beyond state control, President Warren G.

63
00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:55.680
[George] Harding declared martial law and dispatched 2,000 United States Army Infantry troops, accompanied by a squadron of Martin Bomber aircraft commanded by General Billy Mitchell.

64
00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:58.480
[George] For the miners, this changed everything.

65
00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:07.200
[George] Many had worn their olive drab army uniforms to Blair Mountain, and they refused to fire upon the United States flag or their fellow soldiers.

66
00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:19.360
[George] As federal troops arrived on September 3, 1921, the miners quietly laid down their rifles, buried their dead, untied their red bandanas, and slipped away into the hollows.

67
00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:30.080
[George] Nearly a thousand miners were indicted for treason and murder in trials funded directly by the coal operators, though sympathetic juries refused to convict the top Union leaders.

68
00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:37.920
[George] While the battle was a short-term tactical defeat, Blair Mountain became an immortal rallying cry for the American labor movement.

69
00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:40.800
[George] Twelve years later, under Franklin D.

70
00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:50.560
[George] Roosevelt's New Deal, the National Labor Relations Act of 1935 outlawed company gun thugs and guaranteed miners the legal right to organize.

71
00:11:50.560 --> 00:12:01.360
[George] Today, Blair Mountain stands as sacred ground in the Appalachian Mountains, a testament to 10,000 working men who were willing to risk everything for human dignity and freedom.

72
00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:15.280
[George] The heroism of the redneck miners at Blair Mountain permanently transformed American labor consciousness, laying the groundwork for the modern United Mine Workers of America and workplace safety regulations across the industrialized world.

73
00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:27.920
[George] The wooded ridgeline of Blair Mountain was officially added to the National Register of Historic Places, preserving the sacred battleground where ordinary working Americans took a stand against corporate feudalism.

74
00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:36.120
[Rebecca] The red bandanas of Blair Mountain showed that American working people would fight to the death for their dignity and constitutional rights.

75
00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:37.720
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

76
00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:44.920
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we travel to Kentucky for the devastating Great Louisville flood of 1937.

